(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-physiology-master-exam-version-2"]={"title":"Physiology Master Exam — Form B — Units 6, 7, 12 & 13","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"According to the “law of the gut,” a bolus that distends the intestine causes...","choices":["Relaxation on both the upstream and the downstream sides at the same moment","Contraction on the orad (upstream) side and relaxation on the downstream (aboral) side","Contraction ahead of the bolus and relaxation behind it, pushing it backward","No coordinated response of any kind unless the sympathetic nerves fire first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Distension makes the segment behind the bolus contract while the segment ahead relaxes, propelling contents aborally. Simultaneous relaxation, contraction ahead of the bolus, or dependence on sympathetic input all misdescribe this reflex. — GI Physiology, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which statement about insulin and cellular glucose uptake is correct?","choices":["Most glucose uptake uses insulin-independent carriers (e.g., GLUT-1); GLUT-4 in muscle and fat is insulin-dependent","All cellular glucose uptake requires insulin","No glucose transporter responds to insulin at all","Insulin is required for glucose uptake in every tissue of the body, including brain and liver, with no insulin-independent carriers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Most post-absorptive glucose uptake uses insulin-independent transporters like GLUT-1, while GLUT-4 in muscle and adipose is the insulin-dependent carrier. So not all uptake needs insulin, some transporters clearly respond to it, and the liver is not the special case. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 4"},{"q":"Platelets have no nucleus. What is the direct consequence of this?","choices":["They are unable to store calcium for clotting","They cannot adhere to exposed collagen","They cannot contract during clot retraction","They cannot reproduce"],"answer":3,"exp":"Without a nucleus, platelets cannot reproduce. (A) is wrong — platelets do store Ca²⁺. (B) is wrong — they adhere to collagen, aided by von Willebrand factor. (C) is wrong — they contract using actin, myosin, and thrombosthenin. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 32"},{"q":"During quiet (resting) breathing, how is expiration accomplished?","choices":["By active contraction of the diaphragm","Passively, through elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall","By contraction of the abdominal muscles","By active contraction of the external intercostal muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"In quiet breathing the inspiratory muscles simply relax and the stretched lung recoils, expelling air passively — no active expiratory effort is needed. Diaphragm and external-intercostal contraction produce inspiration, and the abdominals are recruited only for forced expiration. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 18"},{"q":"Among the white blood cell types listed in the deck, which functions specifically to activate blood clotting?","choices":["Neutrophils","Eosinophils","Platelets","Lymphocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Platelets (from megakaryocytes) are grouped with the WBCs here and function specifically to trigger blood clotting. Neutrophils and eosinophils are phagocytic/effector granulocytes, and lymphocytes serve the immune system — none of these initiate clotting. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does autonomic input generally affect GI activity?","choices":["Parasympathetic input mainly stimulates, and sympathetic input mainly inhibits","Parasympathetic input inhibits the gut and sympathetic input stimulates it","Both autonomic divisions stimulate the gut to an equal degree","Neither autonomic division has any effect on gut motility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Parasympathetic (vagal/pelvic, ACh) activity generally stimulates motility and secretion, while sympathetic (NE) activity generally inhibits. The other options reverse or deny these effects. — GI Physiology, Slide 25"},{"q":"The complete aerobic oxidation of one glucose molecule yields approximately how many ATP?","choices":["About 2 ATP","About 38 ATP","About 100 ATP","About 12 ATP"],"answer":1,"exp":"Full aerobic oxidation of one glucose yields roughly 38 ATP (glycolysis, pyruvate→acetyl-CoA, the TCA cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation). About 2 ATP is the anaerobic, glycolysis-only yield; 100 and 12 are incorrect. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 23"},{"q":"How many oxygen molecules can a single hemoglobin molecule carry, and why?","choices":["Two — one for each beta chain","Sixteen — four for each of the four chains","Four — one for each iron atom","Eight — one for each globin chain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each hemoglobin has four heme groups, each with one iron atom that binds one O₂ molecule — four O₂ molecules (eight oxygen atoms) per hemoglobin. (D) confuses the four chains with eight molecules; (A) and (B) miscount the heme/iron units. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 21"},{"q":"How does forced expiration differ from quiet expiration?","choices":["It requires surfactant to push air out","It relies solely on passive relaxation of the diaphragm muscle","It makes pleural pressure more negative","It uses active muscle effort, and pleural pressure can become positive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Forced expiration recruits expiratory muscles (e.g., abdominals), which can drive intrapleural pressure positive to expel air rapidly. Passive relaxation and a more-negative pleural pressure describe quiet breathing and inspiration, and surfactant lowers surface tension rather than expelling air. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 18"},{"q":"Compared with a neutrophil, an activated tissue macrophage can...","choices":["Ingest only a single bacterium before it dies off","No longer carry out any phagocytosis at all","Phagocytize far more particles — up to about 100 bacteria","Only ingest viral particles, never whole bacteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Activated macrophages are highly effective phagocytes, able to engulf up to ~100 bacteria and even large particles like damaged red cells. The other options — ingesting only one bacterium, no longer phagocytizing, or ingesting only viruses — all understate or misstate their capacity. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 17"},{"q":"Pepsinogen is secreted primarily by which cells?","choices":["Chief (peptic) cells","Parietal cells","G-cells","ECL cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Chief (peptic) cells secrete pepsinogen. Parietal cells make acid and intrinsic factor secreted by the gastric mucosa, G-cells make gastrin, and ECL cells release histamine. — GI Physiology, Slide 79"},{"q":"The pentose phosphate (hexose monophosphate) shunt is important because it produces...","choices":["large amounts of ATP","NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate for biosynthesis and nucleic acids","ketone bodies for energy","lactate that is exported to the liver for recycling into glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pentose phosphate shunt generates NADPH (for reductive biosynthesis) and ribose-5-phosphate (for nucleic acids), not ATP. It does not make lactate or ketone bodies. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 29"},{"q":"A low plasma transferrin level (poor iron transport) tends to produce which result?","choices":["Enlarged, macrocytic red cells","Iron overload deposited in tissues","Increased oxygen affinity of the hemoglobin molecule","Hypochromic red cells containing too little hemoglobin"],"answer":3,"exp":"With too little transferrin to deliver iron, red cells are made with less hemoglobin than normal — hypochromic anemia. (A) reflects B12/folate problems; (B) is the opposite of poor transport; (C) is unrelated. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 25"},{"q":"Something described as “highly elastic,” like lung tissue, will...","choices":["Resist being stretched and tend to recoil to its original shape","Stretch easily and stay stretched","Have very high compliance","Lose its original shape permanently once it has been stretched"],"answer":0,"exp":"Elasticity is the tendency to return to the original shape after stretch, so a highly elastic structure resists deformation and recoils. Stretching easily or staying deformed describes low elasticity / high compliance — the opposite property. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the correct path of lymph through a lymph node?","choices":["Efferent lymphatics → nodal sinuses → hilus → afferent lymphatics","Blood capillaries → nodal sinuses → node → arterial blood","Afferent lymphatics → nodal sinuses → hilus → efferent lymphatics","Hilus → afferent lymphatics → nodal sinuses → venous blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lymph enters through afferent lymphatics, flows through the medullary sinuses, and exits at the hilus via efferent lymphatics toward venous blood. (A) reverses the flow, and (B) and (D) misroute it. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 22"},{"q":"Gastrin and acetylcholine strongly stimulate acid secretion partly by triggering release of which paracrine agent from ECL cells?","choices":["Somatostatin","Secretin","Motilin","Histamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Gastrin and ACh stimulate ECL cells to release histamine, which powerfully drives parietal-cell acid secretion. Somatostatin and secretin inhibit acid, and motilin governs interdigestive motility. — GI Physiology, Slide 91"},{"q":"Lipoprotein lipase, attached to capillary endothelium, functions to...","choices":["synthesize new triglycerides inside the adipocytes for long-term fat storage","hydrolyze triglycerides in chylomicrons and VLDL, releasing fatty acids to tissues","package cholesterol into HDL","make ketone bodies in the liver"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lipoprotein lipase hydrolyzes the triglycerides carried in chylomicrons and VLDL, freeing fatty acids for uptake by tissues (adipose, muscle, heart). It does not synthesize triglycerides, build HDL, or make ketones. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 69, Slide 13"},{"q":"Hematopoiesis is driven by which two general classes of signals?","choices":["Erythropoietin and thrombopoietin only","Oxygen and carbon dioxide gradients","Vitamin B12 and folate","Growth inducers and differentiation inducers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hematopoiesis is driven by growth inducers (e.g., interleukin-3) and differentiation inducers. (A) names specific hormones, not the general categories; (B) and (C) are conditions/nutrients, not inducer classes. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 9"},{"q":"Because airflow varies with r⁴, a small decrease in airway radius will...","choices":["Sharply increase resistance, since radius is raised to the fourth power","Decrease resistance","Only matter within the alveoli themselves, never in the conducting airways","Barely change resistance"],"answer":0,"exp":"With resistance proportional to 1/r⁴, even slight narrowing greatly increases resistance to airflow. It does not decrease or barely change resistance, and the effect is greatest in the airways (the upper airways carry the major resistance), not the alveoli. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 16"},{"q":"Compared with blood capillaries, lymph capillaries are...","choices":["Much less permeable to particles and large proteins","Completely impermeable to proteins of any size","Highly permeable, allowing particles to enter the lymph","Identical to them in permeability and structure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lymph capillaries are highly permeable relative to blood capillaries, so particles readily enter the lymph (where nodal macrophages can screen them). (A), (B), and (D) misstate this permeability. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which luminal stimulus most strongly triggers CCK release from duodenal I-cells?","choices":["Intact, undigested triglyceride droplets arriving directly from the stomach","Glucose arriving on its own without any fat present","Water entering the duodenum on its own","Fatty acids and monoglycerides (products of fat digestion), plus peptides and amino acids"],"answer":3,"exp":"CCK release is triggered by fat-digestion products (fatty acids, monoglycerides) and by peptides/amino acids — not by intact triglycerides, glucose alone, or water. — GI Physiology, Slide 13"},{"q":"Ketone bodies are produced by the liver mainly...","choices":["from glucose absorbed during a large carbohydrate-rich meal","from fatty acids, to serve as an energy source for other tissues","to store excess dietary protein","only during the well-fed state"],"answer":1,"exp":"The liver makes ketone bodies from fatty acid–derived acetyl-CoA (especially when fat oxidation is high, as in fasting) and exports them as fuel for other tissues. They are not made from glucose in the fed state or used to store protein. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 69, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why are vitamin B12 and folate essential for red blood cell production?","choices":["They form the heme ring of hemoglobin","They carry iron into the mitochondria","They are required for DNA synthesis during rapid cell proliferation","They directly stimulate release of erythropoietin from the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Both vitamins are needed to make thymidine triphosphate and therefore DNA, which rapidly dividing red-cell precursors require. (A) is wrong — heme synthesis is a separate pathway; (B) describes transferrin's role; (D) is wrong — they don't trigger EPO. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 17"},{"q":"Vital capacity is the sum of which volumes?","choices":["Tidal volume + residual volume","Inspiratory reserve volume + the residual volume","Expiratory reserve + residual volume","Inspiratory reserve + tidal volume + expiratory reserve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vital capacity — the maximum air expired after a maximal inspiration — equals IRV + TV + ERV. Any combination that includes residual volume is wrong, because residual volume cannot be exhaled and is not part of vital capacity. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does opsonization accomplish?","choices":["It marks a pathogen (with antibody and complement C3) for easier phagocytosis","It directly lyses the pathogen's outer membrane on contact","It prevents circulating antibodies from binding to the pathogen surface","It neutralizes the active site of a secreted bacterial toxin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Opsonization tags a pathogen with antibody plus complement C3, attracting phagocytes and making ingestion far easier. (B) is complement-mediated lysis, (C) is the opposite of what happens, and (D) describes neutralization. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 15"},{"q":"Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) is notable because it...","choices":["Is the single main stimulus driving contraction of the gallbladder wall","Activates the proteolytic enzymes secreted by the pancreas","Stimulates insulin release in response to oral nutrients (an incretin effect)","Is released only during prolonged fasting between meals"],"answer":2,"exp":"GIP is an incretin: oral nutrients trigger it to enhance insulin release (and it inhibits gastric acid). Gallbladder contraction is CCK's role, zymogen activation is enterokinase/trypsin, and GIP is released after eating, not during fasting. — GI Physiology, Slide 17"},{"q":"How does increasing bile acid excretion (e.g., by fiber that traps bile salts) affect plasma cholesterol?","choices":["It lowers plasma cholesterol, because bile acids are made from cholesterol","It has no effect on plasma cholesterol","It converts the stored cholesterol directly into glucose for use as fuel","It raises plasma cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Excreting bile acids forces the liver to consume cholesterol to make more, lowering plasma cholesterol; fecal loss is the only route of cholesterol removal. So it does not raise cholesterol, have no effect, or make glucose. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 69, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which mechanism is most important for constricting large blood vessels after injury?","choices":["Myogenic contraction from direct damage to the vessel wall","Thromboxane A2 released by platelets","Sympathetic nervous reflexes triggered by local pain fibers","Local autacoids from traumatized tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"For large vessels, direct injury triggers myogenic contraction, the most important constriction mechanism. Thromboxane A2 (B) matters most for small vessels, while nervous reflexes (C) and autacoids (D) contribute but aren't dominant for large vessels. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which lung value CANNOT be measured directly by simple spirometry?","choices":["Tidal volume","Residual volume","Vital capacity","Inspiratory reserve volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Spirometry measures only air that moves in and out, so it cannot capture residual volume (air that never leaves the lungs) — nor the capacities containing it (FRC, TLC). Tidal volume, inspiratory reserve, and vital capacity are all directly measurable. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 31"},{"q":"As a later line of defense, how does the bone marrow respond to a sustained infection?","choices":["It shuts down white-cell production to conserve resources","It increases granulocyte and monocyte production many-fold","It releases mature lymphocytes within minutes","It converts red cells into white cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"The marrow can raise neutrophil and monocyte output 20–50-fold and sustain it for months, with the first new cells released in 3–4 days. (A) is the opposite, and (C) and (D) are physiologically wrong. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which enzyme in saliva begins the digestion of starch in the mouth?","choices":["Pepsin","Trypsin","α-amylase (ptyalin)","Lingual lipase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Salivary α-amylase (ptyalin) starts starch digestion in the mouth. Pepsin digests protein in the stomach, trypsin is a pancreatic protease, and lingual lipase acts on fat, not starch. — GI Physiology, Slide 72"},{"q":"Transamination is the process of...","choices":["removing a phosphate from glucose","oxidizing fatty acids to acetyl-CoA","breaking a peptide bond by adding a molecule of water directly across it","transferring an amino group from one molecule (e.g., glutamate) to an α-keto acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Transamination moves an amino group from a donor (often glutamate) onto an α-keto acid, forming a new amino acid — the core reaction for making non-essential amino acids. Dephosphorylation, peptide-bond hydrolysis, and beta-oxidation are unrelated. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 70, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which step is the rate-limiting step of blood coagulation?","choices":["Conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin","Cross-linking of fibrin by factor XIII","Activation of plasminogen to plasmin","Formation of prothrombin activator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Forming prothrombin activator is the rate-limiting step; once formed, it rapidly converts prothrombin to thrombin. (A) and (B) occur downstream, and (C) is part of clot breakdown, not formation. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 40"},{"q":"According to Fick's law, gas diffusion increases when...","choices":["The partial pressure gradient and surface area are larger","The surface area is smaller","The membrane is thicker","The solubility of the gas in plasma is much lower"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fick's law makes diffusion proportional to the pressure gradient, surface area, and solubility, and inversely proportional to membrane thickness. So a bigger gradient and area speed diffusion, while a thicker membrane, smaller area, or lower solubility slow it. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 62"},{"q":"When an antibody covers the toxic site of a toxin so it can no longer act, this is called...","choices":["Agglutination","Precipitation","Neutralization","Opsonization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Neutralization is the antibody covering the harmful sites of an antigen/toxin. Agglutination clumps particles, precipitation makes a soluble complex insoluble, and opsonization tags a pathogen for phagocytosis. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 52"},{"q":"The final steps of protein digestion (di- and tripeptides to amino acids) occur...","choices":["Only within the lumen of the stomach, before absorption","At the brush border and within the cytoplasm of the mucosal cells","Only in the mouth, by salivary enzymes","In the colon, by bacterial enzymes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Protein digestion finishes at the brush border (oligopeptidases, dipeptidases) and in the enterocyte cytoplasm (di-/tripeptidases). It is not completed in the stomach, mouth, or colon. — GI Physiology, Slide 127"},{"q":"Amino acids that are broken down to acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA are called...","choices":["essential, because they cannot be made","glucogenic, because they form glucose","ketogenic, because they give rise to ketone bodies","non-essential, because they are synthesized"],"answer":2,"exp":"Amino acids degraded to acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA are ketogenic (they can form ketone bodies), whereas those forming glucose or TCA intermediates are glucogenic. 'Essential' vs 'non-essential' refers to whether the body can synthesize them — a different classification. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 70, Slide 18"},{"q":"Clot retraction depends on which cells, acting through what mechanism?","choices":["Platelets contracting via actin, myosin, and thrombosthenin","Red cells contracting by osmotic shrinkage","No cells at all — the retraction is purely spontaneous","White cells engulfing and compacting fibrin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Platelets bound to fibrin contract using actin/myosin/thrombosthenin, tightening the clot; retraction will not occur without platelets. (B), (C), and (D) are incorrect — red cells, spontaneous action, and white cells do not drive retraction. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 43"},{"q":"How does increasing alveolar ventilation affect alveolar PCO₂?","choices":["It raises alveolar PCO₂","It has no effect on alveolar PCO₂","It raises PCO₂ only during exercise","It lowers alveolar PCO₂"],"answer":3,"exp":"Greater alveolar ventilation washes out CO₂ faster than metabolism produces it, so alveolar (and arterial) PCO₂ falls. Ventilation and PCO₂ are inversely related, so raising PCO₂ or “no effect” is incorrect. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 55"},{"q":"Why are helper T-cells described as central to the immune response?","choices":["They directly lyse virus-infected cells using secreted perforins","Their cytokines regulate B-cells, cytotoxic T-cells, and macrophages","They are the only cells in the body that phagocytize bacteria","They form all of the antibodies used in humoral immunity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Helper T-cells secrete cytokines/lymphokines that coordinate B-cells, cytotoxic T-cells, and macrophages — the immune system is nearly paralyzed without them. (A) is the cytotoxic T-cell's job, (C) is a phagocyte role, and (D) is the plasma cell's job. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 57"},{"q":"How do the products of fat digestion (monoglycerides and fatty acids) reach the enterocyte surface for absorption?","choices":["Packaged into micelles with bile salts, which ferry them to the brush border","Bound to intrinsic factor secreted by the gastric mucosa secreted by the stomach","Dissolved freely in the watery chyme and diffusing across on their own","Inside chylomicrons that are assembled within the intestinal lumen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because they are poorly water-soluble, monoglycerides and fatty acids are carried in micelles with bile salts to the enterocyte brush border. They do not simply diffuse, bind intrinsic factor secreted by the gastric mucosa, or ride in lumen-formed chylomicrons — chylomicrons form inside the enterocyte. — GI Physiology, Slide 133"},{"q":"The liver acts as a blood reservoir, normally holding about...","choices":["50% of total blood volume","10% of total blood volume (~450 mL), expandable in heart failure","less than 1% of the total circulating blood volume at any time","no blood between meals"],"answer":1,"exp":"The liver normally holds about 10% of blood volume (~450 mL) and can expand by up to a liter in heart failure or release blood during hemorrhage/exercise. The other figures are far off. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 5"},{"q":"What triggers the intrinsic pathway of coagulation?","choices":["Release of tissue factor from injured tissue","Thrombin cleaving fibrinogen","Contact of blood and factor XII with collagen or a wettable surface","Binding of von Willebrand factor to circulating platelets"],"answer":2,"exp":"The intrinsic pathway starts when blood/factor XII contacts collagen or a wettable surface (e.g., glass), activating factor XII. (A) initiates the extrinsic pathway, (B) is a common-pathway step, and (D) relates to platelet adhesion. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly lists layers a gas crosses in the respiratory membrane, from air to blood?","choices":["Capillary endothelium → basement membranes → alveolar epithelium → surfactant","Interstitial space → surfactant → capillary endothelium","Alveolar epithelium → surfactant → red blood cell","Surfactant/fluid → alveolar epithelium → basement membranes → capillary endothelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Moving from air to blood, a gas crosses the surfactant/fluid layer, the alveolar epithelium, the fused basement membranes and interstitium, and finally the capillary endothelium. The other sequences scramble this air-to-blood order. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 60"},{"q":"A key contrast between acquired and innate immunity is that acquired immunity...","choices":["Is present and fully active from the moment of birth onward","Does not develop until the body is attacked and can take weeks to months","Provides no protection at all against bacterial toxins","Cannot distinguish one antigen from another in any way"],"answer":1,"exp":"Acquired immunity develops only after exposure, requiring weeks to months to mount a specific response. (A) describes innate defenses, and (C) and (D) contradict acquired immunity's specificity and potency. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 35"},{"q":"Once formed, trypsin plays a central role in the intestine by...","choices":["Emulsifying dietary fats within the small intestine","Neutralizing the acidic chyme entering the duodenum","Activating chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and more trypsinogen","Absorbing the di- and tripeptides across the intestinal brush border"],"answer":2,"exp":"Trypsin is the master activator: it converts chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and additional trypsinogen to their active forms. Neutralizing acid is bicarbonate's role, emulsifying fat is bile's, and absorption is the mucosa's. — GI Physiology, Slide 104"},{"q":"The liver's “glucose buffer” function means it...","choices":["stores glucose as glycogen after meals and releases glucose between meals","only stores glucose and never releases it","converts all of the absorbed glucose immediately into stored fat","prevents any glucose from entering the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"As a glucose buffer, the liver stores glucose as glycogen after meals and releases glucose (via glycogenolysis, then gluconeogenesis) between meals, stabilizing blood glucose. The other options describe one-way or nonexistent handling. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is vitamin K's essential role in coagulation?","choices":["It cross-links fibrin monomers into a stable clot","It converts plasminogen into plasmin","It directly activates the platelets arriving at the site of vessel injury","It is needed to carboxylate glutamic acid in prothrombin and factors VII, IX, and X"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vitamin K is required to carboxylate glutamic acid residues on prothrombin and factors VII, IX, X (and protein C), making them functional. (A) is factor XIII's role, (B) is tPA's role, and (C) is unrelated to vitamin K. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 54"},{"q":"The central chemoreceptors are located in the brainstem and respond mainly to...","choices":["Oxygen levels in arterial blood","CO₂ and H⁺","Blood pressure changes","Lung stretch"],"answer":1,"exp":"The central chemoreceptors sense CO₂ and H⁺ (via H⁺ generated in the CSF). Oxygen sensing is the job of the peripheral chemoreceptors, while blood pressure and lung stretch are detected by other receptors. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 96"},{"q":"Where are future T-lymphocytes and B-lymphocytes 'preprocessed'?","choices":["T-cells in the thymus; B-cells in the bone marrow (and fetal liver)","Both of them within the thymus gland","Both of them within the red pulp of the spleen","T-cells in the bone marrow; B-cells in the thymus (and fetal liver)"],"answer":0,"exp":"T-cells are preprocessed in the thymus, while B-cells are preprocessed in the fetal liver and then the bone marrow. (B), (C), and (D) misassign one or both lineages. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein returns excess cholesterol from the tissues back to the liver?","choices":["LDL","VLDL","HDL","Chylomicrons"],"answer":2,"exp":"HDL performs reverse cholesterol transport, returning excess cholesterol from tissues to the liver. LDL delivers cholesterol to cells, VLDL carries liver-made triglyceride to tissues, and chylomicrons carry dietary fat from the gut. — GI Physiology, Slide 139"},{"q":"The amphipathic nature of bile salts allows them to...","choices":["absorb water in the colon","chemically digest the triglycerides into free fatty acids","neutralize stomach acid","emulsify fats and carry lipids to the intestinal wall in micelles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Being amphipathic, bile salts act as detergents that emulsify fat and ferry lipids to the mucosa in micelles. Chemical digestion of triglyceride is lipase's role, and bile salts neither neutralize acid nor absorb colonic water. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 20"},{"q":"Under normal resting conditions, the most important minute-to-minute stimulus driving ventilation is...","choices":["Arterial PCO₂ (and the resulting H⁺)","A small fall in the arterial PO₂","Blood glucose level","Body temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Minute-to-minute ventilation is governed most tightly by arterial PCO₂ and the H⁺ it generates. Modest drops in PO₂ have little effect until PO₂ falls substantially, and glucose and temperature are not the primary respiratory stimulus. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 92"},{"q":"How does the body mainly avoid attacking its own tissues (immunologic tolerance)?","choices":["Self-reactive T- and B-cell clones are induced to undergo apoptosis and are deleted","Self-antigens are permanently hidden away from every immune cell in the body","Antibodies directed against self are neutralized by the complement system","Helper T-cells simply ignore every antigen that they encounter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Tolerance is achieved largely by clonal deletion: clones that bind self-antigens with high affinity are driven into apoptosis (T-cells in the thymus, B-cells in the marrow). (B), (C), and (D) are not the primary mechanism. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 61"},{"q":"Glucose and galactose are absorbed across the intestinal brush border by...","choices":["Simple diffusion down their concentration gradient","Secondary active transport coupled to sodium (SGLT-1)","Endocytosis","Facilitated diffusion that does not use sodium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Glucose and galactose share the Na⁺-coupled SGLT-1 carrier, using the sodium gradient (maintained by the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase) for secondary active transport. They are not absorbed by simple diffusion, sodium-independent facilitated diffusion, or endocytosis. — GI Physiology, Slide 126"},{"q":"The body's largest energy reserve is stored as...","choices":["glucose in the blood","glycogen stored in the liver","triglyceride in adipose tissue","protein in muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Adipose triglyceride is by far the largest energy reserve (~120,000 kcal in a lean adult). Liver glycogen and blood glucose hold only small amounts, and although protein is abundant, little is used as an energy store. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 3"},{"q":"Why does hemoglobin matter so much for oxygen transport?","choices":["Hemoglobin dissolves oxygen directly into the plasma, raising dissolved content greatly","Hemoglobin only carries CO₂, not O₂","Dissolved oxygen carries most of the O₂ in blood","Dissolved O₂ alone (~0.3 ml/100 ml) is far too little; Hb raises capacity to ~20 ml/100 ml"],"answer":3,"exp":"Physically dissolved O₂ is tiny (~0.3 ml/100 ml); binding to hemoglobin lifts capacity to about 20 ml/100 ml, so Hb carries the vast majority of oxygen. It does not dissolve O₂ into plasma, and it carries O₂ as well as some CO₂. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 77"},{"q":"Why is a second exposure to the same antigen met with a stronger, faster response?","choices":["The first exposure eliminated all lymphocytes, so replacements react faster","Memory B- and T-cells from the first exposure persist and respond rapidly","Complement permanently coats the antigen after the first exposure","The antigen becomes weaker over time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Memory B- and T-cells generated during the first exposure remain and mount a rapid, amplified secondary response — the basis for booster immunizations. (A), (C), and (D) do not explain the secondary response. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 48"},{"q":"The final products of carbohydrate digestion that are actually absorbed are...","choices":["Monosaccharides, mostly glucose","Disaccharides such as maltose and lactose","Short glucose polymers","Starch fragments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Carbohydrates must be reduced to monosaccharides (about 80% glucose, plus fructose and galactose) before absorption. Disaccharides, oligosaccharides, and starch fragments must first be split at the brush border. — GI Physiology, Slide 125"},{"q":"Leptin, released from adipose tissue, acts on the hypothalamus to...","choices":["strongly stimulate hunger","reduce appetite and food intake","slow the heart rate","raise blood glucose directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Leptin signals the hypothalamus to suppress appetite and raise energy expenditure as fat stores grow. It does not stimulate hunger (that is ghrelin), raise glucose, or slow the heart. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which change shifts the O₂-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the RIGHT, favoring O₂ unloading?","choices":["Decreased temperature","Increased pH (alkalosis)","Increased CO₂ and H⁺ (lower pH)","Decreased 2,3-BPG"],"answer":2,"exp":"A right shift (lower O₂ affinity, better unloading) results from increased CO₂, increased H⁺/lower pH, higher temperature, and increased 2,3-BPG — the conditions of active tissue. Lower temperature, higher pH, and less 2,3-BPG shift the curve left. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 81"},{"q":"Once inside the enterocyte, absorbed monoglycerides and fatty acids are...","choices":["Broken down further into glycerol and carbon dioxide","Returned to the lumen","Absorbed directly into the portal vein as free fat","Reassembled into triglycerides and packaged into chylomicrons"],"answer":3,"exp":"Inside the enterocyte, absorbed monoglycerides and fatty acids are re-esterified into triglycerides and packaged (with apoproteins) into chylomicrons. They are not oxidized to CO₂ there, sent into portal blood as free fat, or returned to the lumen. — GI Physiology, Slide 132"},{"q":"Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is essential for...","choices":["collagen formation and normal connective tissue","synthesis of the plasma blood clotting factors","calcium absorption","visual pigment formation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vitamin C is required for collagen synthesis, so its deficiency (scurvy) causes connective-tissue breakdown and poor wound healing. Clotting-factor synthesis needs vitamin K, calcium handling needs vitamin D, and visual pigments need vitamin A. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 27"},{"q":"As bicarbonate leaves the red cell in exchange for chloride entering, this exchange is called the...","choices":["Bohr shift","Chloride (Hamburger) shift","Sodium shift","The Haldane shift effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"As bicarbonate leaves the red cell, chloride moves in to preserve electrical neutrality — the chloride (Hamburger) shift. The Bohr and Haldane effects describe how pH/O₂ change gas affinities, and there is no “sodium shift” here. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 86"},{"q":"For defecation to occur, the internal anal sphincter relaxes and then...","choices":["The internal anal sphincter must then contract down again","The pyloric sphincter must open at the same moment","The external anal sphincter must be voluntarily relaxed","Mass movements in the colon must first stop"],"answer":2,"exp":"The internal anal sphincter (smooth muscle) relaxes reflexively, but defecation proceeds only if the external anal sphincter (striated, voluntary) is consciously relaxed. Re-contracting the internal sphincter, opening the pylorus, or stopping mass movements would not allow it. — GI Physiology, Slide 66"},{"q":"Basal metabolic rate (BMR) is measured under which conditions?","choices":["Immediately after exercise","During digestion of a large meal","In a cold environment while actively shivering hard to keep warm","After a 12-hour fast, at rest, in a comfortable temperature, without excitement"],"answer":3,"exp":"BMR is measured under standardized resting conditions — a 12-hour fast, physical and mental rest, and a neutral temperature — so exercise, digestion, and shivering do not inflate it. The other conditions all raise metabolic rate. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 73, Slide 8"},{"q":"If a person hyperventilates and “blows off” CO₂, blood pH will...","choices":["Rise (become more alkaline)","Stay exactly the same","Change only if oxygen also falls","Fall (become more acidic)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Blowing off CO₂ shifts the CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻ equilibrium to the left, lowering H⁺ and raising pH (respiratory alkalosis). Losing CO₂ cannot acidify the blood or leave pH unchanged, and the effect does not depend on oxygen. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 92"},{"q":"The “oxygen debt” repaid after strenuous exercise is used to...","choices":["digest the next meal","convert lactate back to glucose and restore ATP, phosphocreatine, and oxygen stores","produce more lactate","lower the core body temperature back toward its normal set point after exertion"],"answer":1,"exp":"The oxygen debt is the extra O₂ consumed afterward to reconvert lactate to glucose, rebuild ATP and phosphocreatine, and reload hemoglobin/myoglobin and lung oxygen. It is not for digestion, making lactate, or cooling. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 73, Slide 6"},{"q":"How do core and skin temperature differ?","choices":["Both vary widely with the environment","Skin temperature is the one value that the body regulates most tightly of all","Core temperature stays nearly constant, while skin temperature varies with the environment","Core temperature swings more than skin temperature"],"answer":2,"exp":"Deep (core) temperature is held nearly constant (varying <1°F normally), whereas skin temperature rises and falls with the surroundings. The other options misstate which is regulated. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 74, Slide 2"},{"q":"When the body is cold, which response increases heat production?","choices":["Cutaneous vasodilation","Sweating","Inhibition of sympathetic nervous system outflow","Shivering, which can raise heat production several-fold"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the cold, shivering (plus vasoconstriction, sympathetic excitation, and thyroxine) raises heat production up to 4–5-fold. Vasodilation, sweating, and sympathetic inhibition all promote heat loss, not gain. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 74, Slide 14"}]};
